{
  "sourceLanguage" : "en",
  "strings" : {
    "%@" : {

    },
    "%@. It leaves the clipboard in a minute." : {
      "comment" : "Brief notice above the Credentials list in Settings after a secret (a password, a verification code, a hidden value) is copied. First %@ is the usual confirmation, such as “Password Copied”, so the notice reads “Password Copied. It leaves the clipboard in a minute.” WSurf clears the clipboard a minute later, when the vault locks or at quit, unless something else was copied since. Two short sentences."
    },
    "%@ looks like %@, but it is a different website. Add it only if you are sure." : {
      "comment" : "Warning in the toolbar popover after a hand-picked login or code, when the page's website imitates one of the credential's own websites (a look-alike letter, a digit for a letter, a one-letter typo). First %@ is the page's website, second %@ is the credential's website it resembles, both as the address bar draws them (punycode). “Not Now” is then the default button."
    },
    "%@ — %@" : {
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "%1$@ — %2$@"
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "A website’s own Assistant Access setting wins. Private windows always ask. Stop External Connections pauses an app until you resume it." : {
      "comment" : "Note under the Websites section of the Connected apps page. Assistant Access is the name of the per-website setting. Stop External Connections is the exact name of the menu command; Resume is the button on this page."
    },
    "Allow %@ to Use This Page?" : {
      "comment" : "Heading of the sheet that asks whether a paired app may use a page that shows a password or payment field, after it opened the website under the Website access default. %@ is the app’s name, supplied at runtime. Title Case."
    },
    "assistant asks first" : {
      "comment" : "Fragment in the one-line summary under a website, meaning its Assistant Access is set to Ask even if the default would allow more. Lower case: joined with other fragments by a separator, never shown alone."
    },
    "Can’t sign in here: a passkey works only for the website it was made for." : {
      "comment" : "Last line of the Find results on the sheet “Sign in to %@ with a passkey?”, under stored passkeys of other websites. Sentence case."
    },
    "Default (%@)" : {
      "comment" : "Menu item in a website’s Assistant Access choices that removes the website’s own setting so it follows the Website access default. %@ names that default, for example Allow Control, written as the menu spells it."
    },
    "Actions the assistant may take on these websites without asking again, such as purchases or posting." : {
      "comment" : "Caption of the Approved actions page in Settings."
    },
    "Approved actions" : {
      "comment" : "Row title in Settings › Assistant and title of the page it opens: the sensitive actions, such as purchases or posting, that the assistant may take on certain websites without asking again. Sentence case. Replaces Allowed without asking."
    },
    "A website’s own Assistant Access setting wins over this default. Private windows always ask." : {
      "comment" : "Note under the Acting on websites section in Settings. Assistant Access is the name of the per-website setting in the site menu and on a website’s settings page; this default is the Website access row above."
    },
    "Find a passkey…" : {
      "comment" : "Placeholder of the search field on the sheet “Sign in to %@ with a passkey?”. It searches the other passkeys stored in the vault."
    },
    "Follow Website access" : {
      "comment" : "Row on the Connected apps page. Its switch lets paired apps open and use new websites under the Website access default, as the assistant does. Sentence case. Website access is the name of the row in Acting on websites."
    },
    "%@ — Private Browsing" : {

    },
    "No other passkey matches." : {
      "comment" : "Result of the search field on the sheet “Sign in to %@ with a passkey?” when nothing was found. Sentence case."
    },
    "The assistant wants to click “%@” %@. This %@." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "The assistant wants to click “%1$@” %2$@. This %3$@."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "signing in" : {
      "comment" : "Lowercase name of one category of assistant permission, inserted mid-sentence into a list under a website’s name, which reads roughly: purchases and signing in without asking. Deliberately lowercase. The capitalised label Signing in is a separate string."
    },
    "Signing in" : {
      "comment" : "Label of one category of sensitive action in the assistant-permissions list in Settings, covering signing in with another account and giving a website access to an account. A lowercase mid-sentence form of the same phrase exists separately."
    },
    "signs in or gives another website access to your account" : {
      "comment" : "Clause that completes the sentence This … in the dialog asking the user to approve an action the assistant wants to take, for buttons such as Continue with Google or Authorize. Lowercase, third person singular; the implied subject is the action."
    },
    "This page has a password or payment field." : {
      "comment" : "First sentence of the question asking whether the assistant may use a page. It says why the question appears even though the Website access default would allow it. Followed by the usual explanation of Read Only and Allow Control."
    },
    "The assistant reads and uses websites without asking. It still asks before purchases, payments, sending, deleting, and signing in, and on pages with password or payment fields." : {
      "comment" : "Caption of the Website access row when it is set to Allow Control. It lists what the assistant still asks about."
    },
    "The assistant reads websites without asking. It asks before it clicks, types, or submits on a website." : {
      "comment" : "Caption of the Website access row when it is set to Read Only."
    },
    "The assistant asks before it reads or uses each website." : {
      "comment" : "Caption of the Website access row when it is set to Ask. The assistant is the name of the feature."
    },
    "Private windows always ask." : {
      "comment" : "Caption of the Website access row when Settings is open in a private window, and the end of the note under the Acting on websites section. The Website access default never applies in private windows."
    },
    "This page has a password or payment field. %@ will be able to click, type, and navigate on %@. Password and payment fields stay blocked." : {
      "comment" : "Body of the sheet that asks whether a paired app may use a page with a password or payment field. The first %@ is the app’s name and the second is the website’s host, both supplied at runtime. The consequence comes first."
    },
    "%@ wants to open %@." : {
      "localizations" : {
        "en" : {
          "stringUnit" : {
            "state" : "new",
            "value" : "%1$@ wants to open %2$@."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    "%lld paired" : {
      "comment" : "Detail on the Connected apps row in Assistant settings. The number is how many apps are paired."
    },
    "Add a Developer Site" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the alert that asks for a developer site on the Connected apps page. Title Case."
    },
    "Add Site…" : {
      "comment" : "Row on the Connected apps page that opens an alert for typing a developer site. The ellipsis promises the alert. Title Case."
    },
    "Add to This Mac…" : {
      "comment" : "Button on the Vault on your other devices card. Title Case for a command; the ellipsis means a passkey prompt follows."
    },
    "Add to This Mac" : {
      "comment" : "Button in the On your other devices section that adds the profile to this Mac. Title Case for a command."
    },
    "All %lld passwords from the old list are in Credential Manager." : {

    },
    "Allow Developer" : {
      "comment" : "Button in the sheet where the user chooses what a paired app may do in a tab. It grants the Developer tier: development access, typing test passwords the app supplies itself, and no confirmation before purchases, sending, or deleting on developer sites. Title Case. Offered only for a paired app on a developer site."
    },
    "Allow Developer Access in “%@”?" : {
      "comment" : "Heading of the confirmation shown when the Developer profile is set to a profile WSurf did not create for this. The inserted text is the profile’s name."
    },
    "Allow for this private tab" : {

    },
    "Always allow for this website" : {

    },
    "and %lld more entries with notes." : {

    },
    "Apps you paired with WSurf." : {
      "comment" : "Search result description for the Connected apps page."
    },
    "Apps you paired with WSurf. Unpaired apps ask each time they connect." : {
      "comment" : "Caption under the title of the Connected apps page."
    },
    "Ask before opening %@" : {

    },
    "Ask next time" : {

    },
    "Choose another way to continue on the website." : {
      "comment" : "Last line of the passkey sheet when WSurf cannot save a passkey for the website (no vault yet), after the line that says so. It sends the person back to the website’s own other option. Full sentence."
    },
    "Choose another way to sign in on the website." : {
      "comment" : "Last line of the passkey sheet when WSurf holds no passkey for the website, after “No passkey for this website in WSurf.”. It sends the person back to the website’s own other way to sign in (some sites call it “Try another way”). Full sentence."
    },
    "Choose a Developer profile to use this." : {
      "comment" : "Caption on a paired app’s row when its Without asking level is above Off but no Developer profile is chosen, so the level has no effect yet."
    },
    "Connected apps" : {
      "comment" : "Name of the Settings page and section that list apps paired with WSurf over MCP. Sentence case; it is also the text of a link in the External connections page."
    },
    "Connected now" : {
      "comment" : "Caption of a paired app row while the app has a live connection."
    },
    "Copy Configuration" : {
      "comment" : "Button above the client list when pairing is unavailable and the configuration is copied at once. Title Case."
    },
    "Copy Configuration…" : {
      "comment" : "Button above the client list. The ellipsis promises a dialog that asks for a name before the configuration is copied."
    },
    "Create a vault to import the passwords saved before Credential Manager." : {

    },
    "Create Developer Profile" : {
      "comment" : "Row on the Connected apps page that creates a profile named Developer and chooses it as the Developer profile. Title Case."
    },
    "Credential Manager changed. Review the old list again." : {

    },
    "Developer" : {
      "comment" : "Name of the profile WSurf creates for developer work, and the Developer choice in the Without asking menu on a paired app’s row. Title Case."
    },
    "Developer access works only in this profile, so connected apps never use your everyday sign-ins." : {
      "comment" : "Caption under the Developer profile menu on the Connected apps page."
    },
    "Developer Mode for “%@”" : {
      "comment" : "Item in View > Developer that turns Developer mode on or off for one website; a check mark shows it is on. %@ is the website’s host name, such as localhost, and is never translated. Title Case. Quotation marks are curly. Developer mode is a browsing aid for web developers and is not the same as the Developer access of connected apps."
    },
    "Developer Mode for This Website" : {
      "comment" : "Item in View > Developer that turns Developer mode on or off for the website of the current page; a check mark shows it is on. Shown while no page with a website address is open (the item is then dimmed). Title Case. See also Developer Mode for “%@”."
    },
    "Developer profile" : {
      "comment" : "Row title on the Connected apps page for the menu that chooses the only profile where paired apps can work without asking."
    },
    "Developer work" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the section on the Connected apps page that holds the Developer profile and the developer sites."
    },
    "Development" : {
      "comment" : "Choice in the Without asking menu on a paired app’s row, and the name of the access level that adds inspection, diagnostics and device emulation to control. Title Case."
    },
    "Device" : {
      "comment" : "Name shown in the sidebar’s device picker for another device that has no name of its own. A single word. Sentence case."
    },
    "Enter a site like staging.example.com or staging.example.com:8443. Wildcards and address ranges aren’t supported." : {
      "comment" : "Error under the developer sites when the typed site is not an exact host with an optional port."
    },
