      "comment" : "Clause that completes the sentence This … and usually can’t be undone. in the dialog asking the user to approve an action the assistant wants to take. Lowercase, third person singular, and the implied subject is the action being approved."
    },
    "Permissions" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the Websites settings section listing what each website may use (location, camera, microphone, notifications), and the same word in the Settings search index. Plural noun."
    },
    "Permissions for this website" : {
      "comment" : "Tooltip on the permission badge in the address bar when a site has a standing allow or deny on record; clicking opens the permission panel. Sentence case; use “website” rather than “site”."
    },
    "Permissions…" : {
      "comment" : "Opens a panel for location, camera, microphone and notification permissions for this website."
    },
    "Personal" : {

    },
    "Phone" : {

    },
    "Phone from Contacts" : {

    },
    "Pick a Credential for %@" : {
      "comment" : "Title of the Pick Credential… sheet. %@ is the website of the right-clicked field as the address bar draws it (punycode, no sign-in details, port when not 443), e.g. “login.example.com”. Title Case."
    },
    "Pick an option in a dropdown menu." : {
      "comment" : "Caption for the Choose From Menus tool in assistant settings."
    },
    "Pick Credential…" : {
      "comment" : "Item at the top of a web page's context menu when the right-click landed in an editable field, in both engines. Opens a sheet to choose a saved login or verification code for that field. Title Case, ends in an ellipsis because it opens a sheet. Also used inside “Right-click the field and choose Pick Credential… to try again.”"
    },
    "Picture in Picture" : {
      "comment" : "Tooltip on the button that floats the video in a system Picture in Picture window. It is used twice: beside the address field, left of the speaker, for the tab you are looking at, and on the media player in the sidebar. Apple feature name; use the platform glossary. Title Case."
    },
    "Pin" : {
      "comment" : "Pins a sidebar folder or dragged row to the upper pinned section, or puts an extension button back on the toolbar. Imperative verb, one word."
    },
    "Pin This Page" : {
      "comment" : "Command that fixes the page to the tab, so the tab can always return to it and sits in the pinned rows at the top of the sidebar. Three surfaces share it: the context menu of a tab row in the sidebar, the search palette, and the File menu on ⌘D. Title Case. “Pin” is the browser's verb for that anchor; there is no separate bookmarks list."
    },
    "Pink" : {
      "comment" : "Tooltip and accessibility label for one swatch in the colour row of a sidebar folder's context menu, the way the Finder labels its tag colours. A colour name on its own, Title Case; the swatch is 18 points across, so keep it to one word."
    },
    "Place Side by Side" : {
      "comment" : "Menu command that rearranges two stacked pages into two columns. Title Case. Its opposite is Stack Pages, and the menu shows whichever one is available."
    },
    "Plan a day out" : {

    },
    "Play" : {
      "comment" : "Tooltip on the play/pause button in the media player docked at the foot of the sidebar, while playback is stopped. Verb; the dock is narrow, so keep it to one word."
    },
    "Play a song in a tab to see synced lyrics." : {

    },
    "Play chill lofi" : {
      "comment" : "Label on one of three example prompt chips on the start page. “lofi” is the music genre (lo-fi) — keep a form your readers will recognise. The prompt actually sent to the model is separate and stays in English."
    },
    "Play Videos" : {
      "comment" : "Tool name in assistant settings: finding and playing a video."
    },
    "Playing Audio" : {
      "comment" : "Small heading above the list of tabs that are playing sound, which opens when the pointer rests on the speaker button; also the VoiceOver label of that list. Title Case, a noun phrase like a section title."
    },
    "Pop-up windows" : {

    },
    "Pop-up Windows" : {
      "comment" : "Row title in Website Settings for windows a website opens by itself. Title Case, hyphenated as Safari writes it."
    },
    "pop-ups allowed" : {
      "comment" : "One phrase in the summary line under a website in the changed-websites list. Phrases are joined with “ · ”, so this is lowercase and never a sentence."
    },
    "pop-ups blocked" : {
      "comment" : "One phrase in the summary line under a website in the changed-websites list. Phrases are joined with “ · ”, so this is lowercase and never a sentence."
    },
    "Pop-ups never open." : {
      "comment" : "Caption for the Block pop-up choice. One sentence."
    },
    "Postal code" : {

    },
    "posting and sending" : {
      "comment" : "Lowercase name of one category of assistant permission, inserted mid-sentence into a list under a website’s name, which reads roughly: Allowed without asking: deletions and posting and sending. Deliberately lowercase — do not capitalise it, even in languages that capitalise nouns. The capitalised label Posting and sending is a separate string."
    },
    "Posting and sending" : {
      "comment" : "Title Case label of one category of sensitive action in the assistant-permissions list in Settings, covering publishing content and sending messages. A lowercase mid-sentence form of the same phrase exists separately."
